**Idea:** Use the recurrence identity 2·T_m·T_n = T_{m+n} + T_{|m−n|} to build the odd Chebyshev terms directly from T2 = 2z² − 1. This gives T3 = 2·z·T2 − z, T5 = 2·T2·T3 − z and T7 = 2·T2·T5 − T3, with no separate T4 or T6. That cuts ciphertext multiplications from 6 to 4 and brings the multiplicative depth down to about 6 from 8. The c-coefficient products stay as plaintext multiplies on the final terms.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  // z = x / 4 in [-1, 1]
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;

  // T2 = 2 z^2 - 1
  Ciphertext u = z * z;
  Ciphertext T2 = u + u - one;

  // T3 = 2 z T2 - z
  Ciphertext p3 = z * T2;
  Ciphertext T3 = p3 + p3 - z;

  // T5 = 2 T2 T3 - z
  Ciphertext p5 = T2 * T3;
  Ciphertext T5 = p5 + p5 - z;

  // T7 = 2 T2 T5 - T3
  Ciphertext p7 = T2 * T5;
  Ciphertext T7 = p7 + p7 - T3;

  // y = c0 + c1 T1 + c3 T3 + c5 T5 + c7 T7
  Ciphertext y = z * c1;
  y = y + T3 * c3;
  y = y + T5 * c5;
  y = y + T7 * c7;
  y = y + c0;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

**Idea:** The best kernel so far (attempt 1) uses 4 ciphertext multiplications and 6 levels. I could not find a plausible change that drops below 4 ciphertext multiplications for a degree-7 odd polynomial, so I'm keeping that structure. The only change is to accumulate the terms in order of increasing multiplicative depth. This is intended to reduce the level and scale alignment CHEHAB inserts, though I have not measured the effect.

```cpp
